 ],
 "literal": "With the sharp-edged striking harpoon made beautiful by metalwork, / fitted to the tip of a strong shaft of dried bamboo, / having thrown into the difficult-to-bear watery expanse, with pulling force, / the fishermen in curved boats spear the horned fish, / making the short-legged swans that were on the long shore / disperse in white flocks from the seashore grove dense with flowers; / with the lord of such a maritime tract of cool waters and screwpine, / I surely made a friendship that only brings enmity!",
 "translation": "The fishermen in their curved boats throw harpoons—sharp-edged, beautifully crafted, and fitted onto strong shafts of dry bamboo—into the deep, formidable sea. As they pull back with force to spear the horned sharks, the short-legged swans resting on the long shore flee in white flocks from the flower-dense coastal groves. With the lord of this cool, screwpine-fringed shore, I have indeed made a friendship that brings only pain and hostility.",
 "modern": "இரும்புத் தொழிலால் அழகுபெற்ற, கூர்மையான முனையையுடைய எறியும் உளியை, காய்ந்த மூங்கிலின் வலிமையான தண்டுடன் பொருத்தி, தாங்குவதற்கு அரிய கடலில் வீசி, வளைந்த படகுகளையுடைய பரதவர்கள் வலிமையோடு இழுத்துச் சுறா மீன்களைக் குத்துகின்றனர். அதனால், நீண்ட கரையில் இருந்த குட்டைக் கால்களையுடைய அன்னப் பறவைகள், பூக்கள் நிறைந்த கடற்கரைச் சோலையிலிருந்து வெண்மையான கூட்டங்களாக வெருண்டோடுகின்றன. தாழை மலர்கள் நிறைந்த குளிர்ந்த நீர்நாட்டிற்குத் தலைவனான அவனோடு, நான் பகைமையைத் தரும் நட்பை அல்லவா செய்துகொண்டேன்!",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend about her distress over the hero's delay in marrying her. She laments that her love for the lord of the maritime tract has brought her only suffering and hostility from her family and the village. The poem uses a vivid image of the Neytal landscape: fishermen violently spearing horned sharks in the deep sea, which frightens the peaceful white swans on the shore and makes them flee their flowery groves. This image serves as an implied metaphor (uḷḷuṟai) for how the hero's thoughtless actions disrupt the heroine's peaceful life and cause her distress. Key words include 'kōṭṭumīṉ' (horned fish/shark) and 'pakai taru naṭpu' (friendship that brings enmity).",
